 comes with a faster 18 watt USB C  charger compared to 12 watts on the  others however it doesn't get a  headphone jack because Apple wanted to  make it thinner and yes it's crazy thin  compared to the rest but it can actually  Bend if you're not careful with it  unlike the other iPads moving on to  display differences the new air and many  have literally  identical displays but the mini packs  basically the same amount of pixels into  a smaller display so it has the highest  pixels per inch  out of the whole iPad lineup including  the pro and it also comfortably fits in  one hand so it's the lightest and most  portable out of all of these the iPad  pro has basically the same display as  the air but it's better in two ways  a higher 600 nits of brightness compared  to 500 in the addition of pro motion  technology  this basically means that it has a 120  Hertz refresh rate display but it also  has the added advantage of being able to  automatically adjust down to as low as  24 Hertz to match the content you're  viewing which helps save battery life  you can see the difference in fluidity  in this slow motion clip between the air  60 Hertz display and 120 Hertz on the  pro this also greatly reduces the lag on  the Apple pencil and proving the drawing  and note-taking experience thanks to the  120 Hertz display speaking of the Apple  pencil the pro is the only one that  supports this second version which is  quite a bit better because it  magnetically attaches to the side  instantly pairs and wirelessly charges  the other iPads only support the  original Apple pencil which you have to  plug into the Lightning port to charge  the new pencil 2 also has a new double  tap feature which is used to swap  between tools and various applications  back to the displays the budget 10.2  inch iPad is the only one that doesn't  get a very high quality display it's  still decent but it doesn't match the  rest first off it's not fully laminated  which basically means that unlike the  rest the display and the top cover glass  are two separate pieces this leads to it  looking a bit grey lacking deep blacks  like the rest and there's a gap between  the display and the glass which is  noticeable from an angle it's also the  only one that doesn't come with an  anti-reflective coating so you'll notice  reflections a lot more and it only packs  about 450 nits of brightness compared to  500 and 600 on the others  it also doesn't get p3 wide color gamut  like you get on the other eye pads which  makes for a very color accurate display  so as you can see the 10.2 inch iPad is  lacking in the display department now  let's get into compare  the speakers between these iPads  [Music]  [Music]  they all sound pretty decent but the pro  speakers are by far the best because you  get them on both the top and the bottom  which is great for gaming and watching  movies now before we get into  performance let's quickly compare the  cameras the pro gets a 12 megapixel wide  camera compared to an 8 megapixel wide  camera on the rest of the iPads so it's  gotta have a lot more detail it's also  the only one that comes with a 10  megapixel ultra-wide camera which gives  you a unique perspective that you can't  get on the other iPads it's also the  only one that can shoot 4k 60fps video  on either of the cameras compared to  only 1080p 30 video on the other eye  pads on the front side the budget iPad  only gets a 1.2 megapixel camera  compared to seven on the rest so selfies  will look quite a bit worse especially  compared to the iPad pro which also has  portrait mode thanks to the face ID  sensors the iPad pro also gets five  microphones instead of two on the other  eye pads so it sounds much better as  well this is the microphone quality on  the 10.2 inch iPad this is the  microphone quality on the iPad Mini this  is the microphone quality on the iPad  air this is the microphone quality on  the 2020 iPad pro and what's brand-new  this year is the lidar scanner which  